* kinetic energy
	* notation
		* w  = work
		* f  = force
		* d  = distance
		* s  = speed
		* ss = start speed
		* sf = final speed
		* t  = time
		* a  = accelaration
	* work-energy theorem
		* w = f * d = f * (s * t) = f * (((sf + ss) / 2) * ((sf - ss) / a)) = m * (sf^2 - ss^2) / 2
		* motivates the definition of 'kinetic energy' as (m * s^2 / 2)
	* in a purely conservative [path-independent force-field]
		* see
			* the gradient theorem
				\ links-to internal igbeaukwebqyciip
		* it is trivial to derive the conservation of (potential energy + kinetic energy)
